WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.935
-Our first guest tonight

00:00:01.935 --> 00:00:03.102
is an
Academy Award-nominated writer

00:00:03.102 --> 00:00:05.772
and Emmy-nominated actor
who you know from movies

00:00:05.772 --> 00:00:07.607
like "The Big Sick"
and shows like "Silicon Valley."

00:00:07.607 --> 00:00:08.975
He stars
in the highly anticipated

00:00:08.975 --> 00:00:11.778
Marvel movie "Eternals,"
which is in theaters tomorrow.

00:00:11.778 --> 00:00:14.013
Let's take a look.

00:00:14.013 --> 00:00:15.181
[ Creature roars ]

00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:19.151 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:19.151 --> 00:00:21.154
[ Creature roars,
people scream ]

00:00:21.154 --> 00:00:24.790 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:27.360
[ Energy hums ]

00:00:29.061 --> 00:00:32.798
[ Creature shrieks ]

00:00:32.798 --> 00:00:34.867
[ Energy hums ]

00:00:34.867 --> 00:00:37.203
[ Creature shrieks ]

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.776
Please welcome back to the show
our friend Kumail Nanjiani.

00:00:43.776 --> 00:00:45.778
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:45.778 --> 00:00:55.788 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:55.788 --> 00:00:58.891 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:58.891 --> 00:01:01.427
Welcome! It's so good
to see you in person!

00:01:01.427 --> 00:01:04.664
-Oh, my God!
How exciting!

00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:06.766
-Human beings.
-Bunch of people!

00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:10.603
-Human beings.
-It's a bunch of people!

00:01:10.603 --> 00:01:13.973
-We were talking backstage
that one of the problems --

00:01:13.973 --> 00:01:15.608
and there's not many --
but one of the problems --

00:01:15.608 --> 00:01:17.877
-Most of the stuff
has been fixed.

00:01:17.877 --> 00:01:20.346
-From what?
-You said problems.

00:01:20.346 --> 00:01:22.215
Most problems in the world
have been fixed.

00:01:22.215 --> 00:01:24.551
-Yeah, we have very few left.
-Very little.

00:01:24.551 --> 00:01:25.952
Good luck with your show, buddy.

00:01:25.952 --> 00:01:27.286
You're not gonna
have much to talk about.

00:01:27.286 --> 00:01:29.121
-I know! Once --
[ Laughter ]

00:01:29.121 --> 00:01:31.157
You know, you actually --
You and your wife,

00:01:31.157 --> 00:01:33.359
who I saw, your lovely wife,
Emily, backstage.

00:01:33.359 --> 00:01:34.694
You guys, early on
in the pandemic,

00:01:34.694 --> 00:01:37.196
you actually tried to help
people fix their problems

00:01:37.196 --> 00:01:39.999
by being honest about yours.
You did a podcast.

00:01:39.999 --> 00:01:41.633
-Yeah, we did a podcast.
It was called

00:01:41.633 --> 00:01:43.235
"Staying In with
Emily and Kumail."

00:01:43.235 --> 00:01:45.271
It was sort of when
the quarantine first happened.

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:48.241
And she's a therapist,
and I've got a lot of problems,

00:01:48.241 --> 00:01:49.542
so that was --
[ Laughter ]

00:01:49.542 --> 00:01:51.277
That was the dynamic.
-Yeah, it's a good dynamic.

00:01:51.277 --> 00:01:53.112
-Yeah, I was like,
"This is how I'm feeling,"

00:01:53.112 --> 00:01:55.181
and she's like,
"What about this, this, this?"

00:01:55.181 --> 00:01:57.183
And we really thought --
We were like, "We'll do this

00:01:57.183 --> 00:01:59.318
for a couple months,
wrap up the pandemic..."

00:01:59.318 --> 00:02:01.287
[ Laughter ]
"...be out in the world."

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:03.355
Nice, tight eight episodes,
you know?

00:02:03.355 --> 00:02:05.491
[ Laughter ]
And a couple months, like --

00:02:05.491 --> 00:02:07.126
We did it for like
four or five months,

00:02:07.126 --> 00:02:09.061
and then it was like,
"We're out of advice.

00:02:09.061 --> 00:02:11.898
Good luck, everyone.
We are turtling up."

00:02:11.898 --> 00:02:13.199
[ Laughter ]

00:02:13.199 --> 00:02:16.369
"Time to really take care of
what's going on in here."

00:02:16.369 --> 00:02:17.770
[ Laughter ]

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:19.205
You know.
Yeah, but it was good.

00:02:19.205 --> 00:02:20.940
It was good to,
like, have that --

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:23.509
You know, sometimes it's --
You realize you're not having

00:02:23.509 --> 00:02:25.044
deep conversations
with the person

00:02:25.044 --> 00:02:26.212
you're in love with
all the time.

00:02:26.212 --> 00:02:27.813
So It's good to be like,
"This is how I'm feeling.

00:02:27.813 --> 00:02:29.748
How are you feeling?"
-It's interesting.

00:02:29.748 --> 00:02:32.151
-Like, I do feel like it must --
it would be therapeutic

00:02:32.151 --> 00:02:33.986
even if it was just, like,
"Let's do a podcast

00:02:33.986 --> 00:02:36.755
just for you and I
that we can listen to later,"

00:02:36.755 --> 00:02:39.192
but I feel like a married couple
forcing themselves

00:02:39.192 --> 00:02:40.627
to do a podcast
is a good way to listen.

00:02:40.627 --> 00:02:42.928
-I think the best way to have
a conversation with a person now

00:02:42.928 --> 00:02:46.199
is just to put two mics in front
of them and not even record.

00:02:46.199 --> 00:02:48.201
-Yeah.
-I feel like I'm so much more,

00:02:48.201 --> 00:02:51.537
like, personal on podcasts
than I am just talking to people

00:02:51.537 --> 00:02:53.172
I'm actually friends with.

00:02:53.172 --> 00:02:55.507
Like, you take this out of here,
and I'm gonna shut down.

00:02:55.507 --> 00:02:57.377
-Absolutely.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:57.377 --> 00:02:59.145
And then,
if it ever gets awkward,

00:02:59.145 --> 00:03:01.647
you can just do, like,
a sponsor plug.

00:03:01.647 --> 00:03:03.349
-Yeah, exactly.
[ Both laugh ]

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:06.452
"MeUndies --
They're so comfortable.

00:03:06.452 --> 00:03:08.487
Get them."
Unless -- do you have a rival

00:03:08.487 --> 00:03:09.889
underwear thing
that sponsors you?

00:03:09.889 --> 00:03:11.891
-We don't. We've been dying
to get an underwear sponsor.

00:03:11.891 --> 00:03:14.027
-Today, buddy.
We're just dead-end city.

00:03:14.027 --> 00:03:15.495
-Well, you just got one today,
buddy.

00:03:15.495 --> 00:03:17.396
[ Laughter ]

00:03:17.396 --> 00:03:19.532
-We were saying
one of the tricks of doing press

00:03:19.532 --> 00:03:22.468
for a movie like this is,
you know, not because people

00:03:22.468 --> 00:03:24.871
are doing a bad job but they ask
you a lot of the same questions.

00:03:24.871 --> 00:03:27.306
So I wanted to try to ask you
about something in your life

00:03:27.306 --> 00:03:30.209
right now that I feel like maybe
no one has asked you about.

00:03:30.209 --> 00:03:32.111
There is a Cricket World Cup
happening right now.

00:03:32.111 --> 00:03:35.581
-There is.
-And Pakistan had a huge win.

00:03:35.581 --> 00:03:37.884
-Oh, we're kicking ass.
[ Scattered cheers ]

00:03:37.884 --> 00:03:40.252
-There we go.
I feel like that might have been

00:03:40.252 --> 00:03:41.520
more applause for you
than the actual --

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:45.057
-Well, I want to ask.
Is Pakistan in the house?

00:03:45.057 --> 00:03:46.626
-Yeah!
-Yes! There it is!

00:03:46.626 --> 00:03:48.895
-There we go.
-Love it.

00:03:48.895 --> 00:03:50.830
Yeah, we are undefeated.
We're winning --

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:53.566
-Is it a hard sell
to try to get Americans

00:03:53.566 --> 00:03:56.769
interested or into cricket?
-Well, I feel like --

00:03:56.769 --> 00:03:58.771
You know, people ask me
to explain cricket --

00:03:58.771 --> 00:04:00.440
have asked me
to explain cricket to them.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:03.042
First thing you have to do is,
you have to forget baseball.

00:04:03.042 --> 00:04:04.910
-Right.
-Like, you really have to.

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:08.147
What happens is,
whenever I start explaining

00:04:08.147 --> 00:04:11.484
cricket to them, and I start
using terms like "googly"...

00:04:11.484 --> 00:04:13.552
-Yeah.
-..."silly mid on,"

00:04:13.552 --> 00:04:15.521
"silly mid off" --
these are real terms.

00:04:15.521 --> 00:04:17.022
-Yeah.
-They just, like,

00:04:17.022 --> 00:04:19.225
start making fun of me.
-Sure.

00:04:19.225 --> 00:04:21.226
-So whenever anybody asks me
to explain cricket,

00:04:21.226 --> 00:04:23.429
I know about 45 seconds
from now,

00:04:23.429 --> 00:04:25.231
I'm just gonna get dunked on.
-Right.

00:04:25.231 --> 00:04:27.333
-It's just a setup
to make fun of me.

00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:28.834
-[ Laughs ]
-Do you know cricket at all?

00:04:28.834 --> 00:04:32.171
-I had an Australian roommate,
so I watched some cricket,

00:04:32.171 --> 00:04:35.808
and I thought
you had to drink a fair amount

00:04:35.808 --> 00:04:39.078
to care enough to stay.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:39.078 --> 00:04:42.180
-I mean, those games
used to last five days.

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:45.017
-It's not dumber than baseball,
it should be noted.

00:04:45.017 --> 00:04:47.653
You just grow up with baseball,
so you --

00:04:47.653 --> 00:04:49.555
-You're not gonna goad me
into saying anything negative

00:04:49.555 --> 00:04:52.392
about baseball in America.
-Okay. That's very wise.

00:04:52.392 --> 00:04:56.094
-I'm just starting to make it.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:56.094 --> 00:04:59.565
-Very, very, very wise.
-Yeah.

00:04:59.565 --> 00:05:03.035
Baseball -- what a sport.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:03.035 --> 00:05:06.772
-Now --
Oh, man, baseball -- that is --

00:05:06.772 --> 00:05:09.575
Now that's a game.
-So believable.

00:05:09.575 --> 00:05:13.379
-I can do compliments without
really saying anything positive

00:05:13.379 --> 00:05:14.580
about baseball
-Yeah. It was --

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:16.282
-You sold it very well.
-Baseball!

00:05:16.282 --> 00:05:19.751
Ooh, baby, that's a day!
[ Laughter ]

00:05:19.751 --> 00:05:22.154
-You should do ads for baseball
during your podcast.

00:05:22.154 --> 00:05:25.258
-Yeah.
"Baseball -- uh, play it."

00:05:25.258 --> 00:05:27.860
[ Laughter ]

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:30.663
"Baseball -- what do you think
of the number 9?

00:05:30.663 --> 00:05:33.733
There's gonna be two innings
of those numbers.

00:05:33.733 --> 00:05:35.000
Have fun."
-Oof.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:36.368
Got a little over your skis --

00:05:36.368 --> 00:05:37.736
You got over your skis
on that one.

00:05:37.736 --> 00:05:39.839
[ Laughter ]
-We can edit it.

00:05:39.839 --> 00:05:41.007
-No, no, no.
We're gonna leave that all in.

00:05:41.007 --> 00:05:42.074
In fact,
we're gonna make it longer.

00:05:42.074 --> 00:05:43.142
We're gonna slow it down
when you say it.

00:05:43.142 --> 00:05:46.746
-You just lost
the MeUndies sponsor.

00:05:46.746 --> 00:05:49.782
-Hey, so the "Eternals,"
this is maybe a lesser-known

00:05:49.782 --> 00:05:51.750
part of
the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:54.720
-You mean more people
know Spider-Man than Eternals.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:56.188
-I didn't want to say it to you,

00:05:56.188 --> 00:05:57.823
but I do believe that
to be true.

00:05:57.823 --> 00:05:59.559
-I disagree.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:59.559 --> 00:06:02.161
-Now, I think one of
the first times we met --

00:06:02.161 --> 00:06:04.163
this is a true story --
was in the basement

00:06:04.163 --> 00:06:07.467
of a comic-book store.
-Yeah, so I used to do --

00:06:07.467 --> 00:06:09.435
that Emily used to produce --
my friend Jonah Ray and I --

00:06:09.435 --> 00:06:12.638
hosted in the back
of a comic-book store,

00:06:12.638 --> 00:06:13.872
called Meltdown Comics.

00:06:13.872 --> 00:06:16.208
We hosted
a stand-up comedy show.

00:06:16.208 --> 00:06:19.411
For six years, we did three
seasons on Comedy Central.

00:06:19.411 --> 00:06:20.646
It was super fun.
So I've been, like,

00:06:20.646 --> 00:06:24.283
a comic-book fan, nerd fan
since I was a kid,

00:06:24.283 --> 00:06:26.218
but all the way through.
Like, I never stopped.

00:06:26.218 --> 00:06:27.820
You're a big comic-book fan.
-I am, yeah.

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:29.388
-Yeah.
Did you know Eternals?

00:06:29.388 --> 00:06:31.657
-I didn't.
I have to admit that I didn't.

00:06:31.657 --> 00:06:34.226
I grew up more of a DC guy
than a Marvels guy --

00:06:34.226 --> 00:06:36.229
Marvel guy -- excuse me.
-Yeah, you don't even know

00:06:36.229 --> 00:06:38.164
the name of the company.
-Marvels.

00:06:38.164 --> 00:06:40.399
-Marvels?
It's like "Carvel," right?

00:06:40.399 --> 00:06:42.067
-It's like "Carvel."
Yeah, we have a --

00:06:42.067 --> 00:06:45.671
we have our own whale character.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:45.671 --> 00:06:48.307
I can't think of it.
-Fudgie the Whale, right?

00:06:48.307 --> 00:06:52.478
-Marvel must have some--
Like, they have King Shark,

00:06:52.478 --> 00:06:54.981
DC does.
We have to have some sort of

00:06:54.981 --> 00:06:57.483
fish-based superhero.
-Yeah, that's true.

00:06:57.483 --> 00:06:59.685
-Do you edit the show?
I think you might.

00:06:59.685 --> 00:07:01.820
[ Laughter ]
Just this one.

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:03.589
-We've always said,
"No matter what..."

00:07:03.589 --> 00:07:05.424
and I appreciate that
you're really testing us,

00:07:05.424 --> 00:07:06.658
but we're not going to.
-Kumail's fish ref

00:07:06.658 --> 00:07:09.995
that went nowhere.
We're just gonna show all of it.

00:07:09.995 --> 00:07:12.698
-Kumail, we saw a little
on the clip, but I have to say,

00:07:12.698 --> 00:07:14.033
you know, I think one of
the most exciting things is

00:07:14.033 --> 00:07:15.501
you get to cast
in a superhero movie,

00:07:15.501 --> 00:07:16.969
but then the first time you get
to see yourself in uniform,

00:07:16.969 --> 00:07:18.603
I mean,
you look outside standing.

00:07:18.603 --> 00:07:21.874
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Yeah.

00:07:21.874 --> 00:07:24.743
Look at that.

00:07:24.743 --> 00:07:26.178
Pretty great.
-Pretty great.

00:07:26.178 --> 00:07:29.014
-Yeah, it looks good.
You know, you put it on

00:07:29.014 --> 00:07:31.617
for the first time, and you
stand up a little straighter

00:07:31.617 --> 00:07:34.420
or you feel cool.
But I put it on --

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:36.355
And, you know,
it's a bunch of pieces.

00:07:36.355 --> 00:07:37.957
Even though
it is pretty comfortable,

00:07:37.957 --> 00:07:39.659
it's a bunch of pieces.
People have to help you.

00:07:39.659 --> 00:07:42.061
You can't just get into it.
-Right.

00:07:42.061 --> 00:07:44.363
-There's a crime.
Get your team --

00:07:44.363 --> 00:07:46.899
Get your wardrobe team
to show up.

00:07:46.899 --> 00:07:48.167
It's gonna take us
10 to 12 minutes.

00:07:48.167 --> 00:07:49.901
-So, right.
If they call you and say,

00:07:49.901 --> 00:07:51.870
"Hey, there's a crime,"
like, what are you telling them?

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:54.807
45 minutes, minimum?
-I mean, depends.

00:07:54.807 --> 00:07:56.775
How's Uber doing, right?
-Right.

00:07:56.775 --> 00:07:59.578
Uber's been really slow.
My guy can't fly.

00:07:59.578 --> 00:08:01.614
Once he gets there, he can
shoot stuff from his hands,

00:08:01.614 --> 00:08:03.616
but you got to take him
to the location.

00:08:03.616 --> 00:08:06.318
[ Laughter ]

00:08:06.318 --> 00:08:10.122
-So, he's -- [ Laughing ]

00:08:10.122 --> 00:08:13.091
Yeah.
So, he's --

00:08:13.091 --> 00:08:15.327
You put the costume on,
you feel pretty great.

00:08:15.327 --> 00:08:16.762
But, you know,
it's a bunch of pieces,

00:08:16.762 --> 00:08:19.131
and so you can't really go to
the bathroom once you put it on.

00:08:19.131 --> 00:08:21.400
So, in the morning,
I would put it on,

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:24.503
and if I was wearing it all day,
I would, like, not have water,

00:08:24.503 --> 00:08:26.905
not have coffee, like,
be careful, right?

00:08:26.905 --> 00:08:28.640
And then, a couple of weeks,
I've been shooting

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:31.710
in the costume, and I was --
my buddy Brian Tyree Henry,

00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:34.113
who's in the movie, who's
absolutely amazing in the movie,

00:08:34.113 --> 00:08:36.948
he was drinking coffee
in his suit, and I was like,

00:08:36.948 --> 00:08:40.552
"How do you -- How do you
deal with the bathroom piece?"

00:08:40.552 --> 00:08:43.756
And he said, "There's a zipper."
And that's when I found out

00:08:43.756 --> 00:08:45.390
I'd been putting
the pants on backwards.

00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:51.430
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:52.832
It's my first time
being a superhero.

00:08:52.832 --> 00:08:54.300
-It's your
first superhero movie.

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:56.836
Classic first-time mistake.
-I just got here.

00:08:56.836 --> 00:09:00.506
-Your --
You wrote about your family

00:09:00.506 --> 00:09:02.074
in the wonderful film
"The Big Sick,"

00:09:02.074 --> 00:09:04.110
and I'm wondering, has your
family seen this film, yet?

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:07.946
-They just saw it Monday night
at a screening in New York.

00:09:07.946 --> 00:09:10.749
-And I think we've talked
in the past, your parents feel

00:09:10.749 --> 00:09:13.752
pretty free to give you
feedback, yes?

00:09:13.752 --> 00:09:16.588
-They do.
And it's been --

00:09:16.588 --> 00:09:18.991
It's changed,
because there was --

00:09:18.991 --> 00:09:22.694
there was clear feedback,
very honest.

00:09:22.694 --> 00:09:24.897
And then I kind of
had a talk with them.

00:09:24.897 --> 00:09:27.866
I was like, "You know, it's okay
if you don't like something.

00:09:27.866 --> 00:09:30.469
Just tell me you like it.
That's all I want from you."

00:09:30.469 --> 00:09:31.870
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:31.870 --> 00:09:34.373
-And then sometimes they will be
like, "We are now performing

00:09:34.373 --> 00:09:36.409
the action
you have requested of us."

00:09:36.409 --> 00:09:39.845
[ Laughter ]
"It was good."

00:09:39.845 --> 00:09:41.246
Which I do appreciate.
-Yes.

00:09:41.246 --> 00:09:42.314
-I do want that honesty,

00:09:42.314 --> 00:09:44.717
just not from my parents
all the time.

00:09:44.717 --> 00:09:47.386
-But then, they really,
really love this movie.

00:09:47.386 --> 00:09:49.187
Like, I didn't have
to prompt them or anything.

00:09:49.187 --> 00:09:50.722
I finished the movie --
You know, we finished it,

00:09:50.722 --> 00:09:53.025
and I was just sitting there,
and my mom reached over,

00:09:53.025 --> 00:09:55.061
and she was like,
"That was really good!"

00:09:55.061 --> 00:09:57.530
She was like,
"I don't like these movies,

00:09:57.530 --> 00:10:00.066
but that one was really good!"
[ Laughter ]

00:10:00.066 --> 00:10:02.134
-That's when it's very nice
that your parents

00:10:02.134 --> 00:10:04.070
are terrible actors, that
you can tell the difference.

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:05.837
-I could tell the difference,
yeah.

00:10:05.837 --> 00:10:08.207
They were very genuine.
Like, my dad, you know --

00:10:08.207 --> 00:10:09.809
I knew my dad
was going to love it.

00:10:09.809 --> 00:10:13.646
Like, he's sort of all-in on
MCU, but my mom isn't as much,

00:10:13.646 --> 00:10:15.347
and she really, really loved it.

00:10:15.347 --> 00:10:18.617
I could hear her laughing
the whole time.

00:10:18.617 --> 00:10:21.954
They just had a great time.
It was really exciting.

00:10:21.954 --> 00:10:24.489
-It is fantastic,
and I'm so happy

00:10:24.489 --> 00:10:25.957
to have you here in person.
Congrats on the film.

00:10:25.957 --> 00:10:27.926
-It's so good to be here.
My God.

00:10:27.926 --> 00:10:30.529
-You guys,
that's Kumail Nanjiani.

00:10:30.529 --> 00:10:32.264
"Eternals"
is in theaters tomorrow.

00:10:32.264 --> 00:10:34.100
We'll be right back
with Jonathan Majors.

